{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '332,357p' paper.tex; jq -r '.claims|to_entries[]|\\\"\\\\(.key): \\\\(.value.claim)\\\\n ids=\\\\(.value.source_ids|join(\\\",\\\")) refs=\\\\(.value.evidence_refs|join(\\\";\\\"))\\\"' claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Selection Sensitivity and Incomplete Settings}\nThe development evidence contains related common configurations with learning-rate/decay/initialization triples $(0.003,1.0,1.0)$, $(0.003,1.0,0.5)$, and $(0.006,0.5,0.5)$. These are sensitivity conditions, not independent scientific replications of the final comparison. The first two differ in initialization at fixed learning rate and decay. The last differs in learning rate and decay while preserving the nominal product $\\eta\\lambda$ relative to the studied condition. Equal products do not imply equal adaptive updates or effective regularization. Their full observations remain in the registry, and the controller snapshots are supplied with the code.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/0/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/1/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/2/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThe faster-update configuration has incomplete recorded coverage. A missing record cannot be used to claim that its accuracy was zero or that it lost a measured comparison. Its reported aggregate execution-related rates do not identify a cause of scientific failure. We do not promote its nominal shrinkage relationship into a completed positive-decay factorial intervention, a post-fit exposure match, or a clock-collapse result. The measured reference conditions within retained cells provide the appropriate local baseline; no unavailable external baseline is reconstructed from a selection score.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/2]]\n\n\\subsection{Censoring, Denominators, and Uncertainty}\nThe normalized capped delay is $r=\\max(0,\\gen-\\fit)/H$ when both onsets are observed, and $r=1$ otherwise. The observed width-128 final values are $17300/24000$ and $19000/24000$. Assigning the same cap to a missing cell in an aggregate is a weighting convention, not evidence that its generalization was censored. Likewise, a missing final endpoint receives no measured accuracy in our per-seed tables or figures.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L1008]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThe supplied final design-weighted summaries are delayed-event fraction $0.222222$, endpoint-accuracy functional $0.488133$, and normalized capped-delay functional $0.945833$. The first uses nine planned cells, the second includes missing-cell zero weighting, and the third assigns missing cells the cap. They are not, respectively, a population event probability, the mean accuracy of the five observed models, or an observed mean generalization time. For comparison, the descriptive mean of the five retained studied endpoints is $0.878640$. We retain these semantics for auditability, not as stronger headline results.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.grokking_incidence]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.mean_final_accuracy]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.restricted_mean_delay]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/final_observed_endpoint_mean]]\n\nThe two-sided exact binomial intervals use beta quantiles: for $k$ events among $n$ retained seeds, the lower endpoint is zero for $k=0$ and otherwise $B^{-1}_{k,n-k+1}(0.025)$; the upper endpoint is one for $k=n$ and otherwise $B^{-1}_{k+1,n-k}(0.975)$. This calculation is exploratory and conditional on retained records. It neither assumes that unavailable records are observed non-events nor establishes that missingness is ignorable. No survival-model estimate, significance test, or mechanistic confidence interval is reported.\n% @evidence[[scripts/build_artifacts.py]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/cells]]\n\n0: The final evaluation completed, did not qualify, and has a false qualification decision; the supplied diagnostics do not identify the cause.\n ids=final.qualification.status,final.qualification.outcome,final.qualification.correct refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation;paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome\n1: Five of nine planned final width-seed records are retained, with coverage 3/2/0 at widths 32/128/256; absent records are not measured non-events or accuracy losses.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes,final.metric.complete_episode_fraction refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n2: The two retained width-128 studied final records show delayed events; all three width-32 held-out onsets are right-censored at 24000 updates.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n3: Development fitting onsets in seed order 101/211 are 2200/2800 at width32, 300/300 at width128, and 200/200 at width256.\n ids=result.top_program_01 refs=paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n4: Development widest-minus-narrowest paired fit differences are -2000 and -2600 updates; fitting is earlier but endpoint accuracy is nonmonotone in width in both retained seeds.\n ids=result.top_program_01 refs=paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/summary.json#/paired/development_256_minus_32\n5: Development studied endpoint means plus sample SD are 74.68+/-4.47, 29.33+/-3.68, and 39.35+/-3.49 percent at widths32/128/256, over two seeds each.\n ids=result.top_program_01 refs=paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/summary.json#/cells\n6: The task is modulo97 addition, learned by two summed embeddings, GELU, and linear output, with widths32/128/256 and 9441/37473/74849 parameters.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L801;derived/observations.json#/rows\n7: Development/final pools have4704/4705 disjoint pairs with per-seed train/evaluation sizes2822/1882 and2823/1882, respectively; measured results cover one modular operation.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes,result.top_program_01 refs=source_evidence/task/resources/search/dataset.json;source_evidence/task/resources/final/dataset.json;paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n8: The studied learning-rate/decay/initialization configuration is(0.003,1,0.5), common to all retained widths and seeds; reference(0.001,1,1) differs in two settings.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L795;source_evidence/controllers/studied/src/program.py#L37\n9: Zero decay changes only the studied decay to0; norm matching rescales to the same-seed width128 global norm, not to equal capacity or function.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L795;source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L821\n10: Width128 norm-matched scientific curves and initial/final hashes duplicate the studied records; they are not additional independent delayed events.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n11: Development uses seeds101/211 at6000 updates; final uses60613/70717/80821 at24000, with checkpoints every100 updates; pools and seeds differ along with duration.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes,result.top_program_01 refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n12: Training-fit/held-out onsets require thresholds0.99/0.95 sustained for three consecutive checkpoints, timestamped at the first; a delayed event requires at least1000 updates between onsets.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L1001;paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n13: Final width128 seeds60613/70717 fit at300/200 and generalize at17600/19200; observed delays are17300/19000 updates; endpoints98.353/97.768 percent.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/observations.json#/rows\n14: Final width32 seeds60613/70717/80821 fit at2600/2400/3000, have censored held-out onset at24000, and endpoints80.925/88.842/73.433 percent.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/observations.json#/rows\n15: The retained seed-paired final width128-minus32 endpoint differences are17.428 and8.927 percentage points, not the planned256-minus32 contrast.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/summary.json#/paired/final_128_minus_32\n16: Conditional on retained final records, event counts2/2 and0/3 have exact95-percent binomial intervals[0.158,1] and[0,0.708]; these exploratory intervals neither resolve missingness nor establish population or mechanistic validity.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/summary.json#/cells;scripts/build_artifacts.py\n17: Retained final no-decay width32 endpoints0.478--0.797 percent and width128 endpoints0 percent coexist with fit onsets1300--1600 and200, respectively; this is a finite-horizon configuration contrast only.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/observations.json#/rows\n18: Development norm-matched width32 endpoints70.510/56.164 percent exceed width128 endpoints31.934/26.727 in both seeds; scalar norm matching does not remove that ordering.\n ids=result.top_program_01 refs=paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n19: Retained final reference endpoints54.570--65.037 percent atwidth32 and80.659--84.272 atwidth128 show no sustained held-out threshold crossing; these are multi-setting reference comparisons.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/observations.json#/rows\n20: Final event fraction0.222222 is design-weighted over nine cells, mean endpoint functional0.488133 includes missing-cell zero weighting, and capped delay0.945833 includes missing-cell cap weighting; these are not population event rates, observed endpoint means, or uncensored mean event times.\n ids=final.metric.grokking_incidence,final.metric.mean_final_accuracy,final.metric.restricted_mean_delay refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics;source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L1066\n21: The descriptive mean of the five observed final studied endpoints is0.878640, distinct from the failure-weighted registry functional.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/summary.json#/final_observed_endpoint_mean\n22: Related development settings are(0.003,1,1),(0.003,1,0.5),(0.006,0.5,0.5); the faster setting has an unavailable width256 seed211 record, not a measured zero or known failure cause.\n ids=result.top_program_01,result.top_program_02 refs=paper_data.json#/all_programs/0/evaluation_evidence/episodes;paper_data.json#/all_programs/1/evaluation_evidence/episodes;paper_data.json#/all_programs/2/evaluation_evidence/episodes\n23: The evaluated source uses CPU float32, full-batch AdamW, deterministic algorithms, at most two CPU threads and one inter-op thread; requirements pin numpy2.1.3 and torch2.5.1+cpu.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L765;source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L781;source_evidence/task/requirements.txt\n24: The readable final evaluator digest matches the registry evaluator digest; hash agreement is provenance, while code contents support architecture and optimizer details.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=derived/source_manifest.json;source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py;paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluator_sha256\n25: Recorded selected-development seed evaluation durations are981/754 seconds, and final seed totals3199/2794/3488 seconds after rounding; incomplete final coverage prevents extrapolating complete-grid runtime.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes,result.top_program_01 refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/summary.json#/runtime_by_scope_seconds\n26: All five retained final studied endpoints have training accuracy1.0, with different residual train cross-entropies; initial/final global norms increase in every record, including delayed-event records. Table norms/losses are individual measured endpoints, not a norm-trajectory mediation test.\n ids=final.evidence.episodes refs=paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes;derived/observations.json#/rows;derived/norm_loss_records.tex\n27: Power et al. document delayed generalization on small algorithmic datasets.\n ids=ref.arxiv.2201.02177 refs=source_evidence/literature/2201.02177_opening.txt\n28: Nanda et al. reverse-engineer a Fourier-based modular-addition computation in small Transformers; our architecture and measurements do not establish the same mechanism.\n ids=ref.arxiv.2301.05217 refs=source_evidence/literature/2301.05217_opening.txt\n29: Liu et al. relate grokking to loss landscapes as a function of weight norm.\n ids=ref.arxiv.2210.01117 refs=source_evidence/literature/2210.01117_opening.txt\n30: Varma et al. propose differing learning speeds and norm efficiencies of memorizing and generalizing circuits.\n ids=ref.arxiv.2309.02390 refs=source_evidence/literature/2309.02390_opening.txt\n31: Kumar et al. study lazy-to-rich dynamics, including polynomial regression exhibiting grokking without regularization.\n ids=ref.arxiv.2310.06110 refs=source_evidence/literature/2310.06110_opening.txt\n32: Davies et al. connect grokking and double descent through pattern-learning speeds and capacity variation.\n ids=ref.arxiv.2303.06173 refs=source_evidence/literature/2303.06173_opening.txt\n33: Nakkiran et al. document nonmonotone generalization as model size or duration varies; the present narrow sweep does not establish double descent.\n ids=ref.arxiv.1912.02292 refs=source_evidence/literature/1912.02292_opening.txt\n34: AdamW decouples weight decay from adaptive loss-gradient updates; nominal shrinkage is not a measured complete dynamical clock.\n ids=ref.arxiv.1711.05101 refs=source_evidence/literature/1711.05101_opening.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
